---
/**
 * About Alan, with the talk, family origin and dog photographs. Written to
 * the website-page playbook: About is where Alan speaks most as himself, in
 * the talking register, with claims kept to the ledger's facts, and it ends
 * with the routes, plainly: the 101, the courses, the free hour.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Signoff from "../components/Signoff.astro";
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import zelda from "../assets/photos/zelda.jpg";
import mmnTalk from "../assets/photos/mmn-talk.jpg";
import parents from "../assets/photos/stuart-alison.jpg";

const contactSubject = "Hello";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Alan Harman-Box - burningsuit"
  description="I'm Alan Harman-Box, and burningsuit is me. I teach Power BI and AI to people whose job isn't Power BI or AI, and work alongside teams on their own reports."
  contactSubject={contactSubject}
>
  <Fragment slot="footer-invitation"
    >If any of this sounds like what you're dealing with, I'd like to hear about it</Fragment
  >

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="enter appear-300">Power BI and AI for everyone <em>else</em>.</h1>
      {/* ALAN: your own words, lightly tidied; check they still sound like you. */}
      <p class="lead enter appear-450">
        I'm Alan, and burningsuit is me. There's a lot of noise about AI and Power BI at the moment, and most of it is aimed at people who are already knee-deep in agents. I'm interested in everyone else: the people who want to do their job well and would rather not turn into a nerd to do it. That's fine by me. I'm happy to be the nerd in the room so you don't have to be.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="capable">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="capable" data-reveal>I start from what people can do</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          I have a stubborn belief that, with the right teaching and a bit of encouragement, almost anyone can learn almost anything. So I don't turn up assuming a team is behind, or that the people are the problem. Most teams I meet are more capable than they've been given credit for. They're busy with the day job, wary of the latest thing, and short of someone to think it through with.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          The rest of the site has the courses, how I work with teams on their own reports, and <a href="/work/">a few case studies</a>. And I'm on <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/alan-harman-box"
          target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>, if that's where you check people out.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="human">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="human" data-reveal>The part that isn't technical</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          I can do the nuts and bolts of Power BI, and I've done them for a long time. But that's rarely what makes the difference. What matters more is whether people who are busy, and a bit sceptical, feel like having a go, and whether your team ends up thinking I understand how they work, rather than that someone turned up to tell them they've been doing it wrong.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          I've spent about five years working with organisations getting to grips with Power BI and Fabric, and a decade or so before that in software delivery: large systems, some of it for government. I've also given a talk called <a href="/writing/we-are-all-middle-management-now/">We're all middle management now</a> at the Bristol and Manchester Power BI user groups and at DataSwindon. It's about being responsible for work you've handed to something that can't be held responsible for it. What's stuck with me through all of it is that these projects succeed or fail on the people side, well before the technology comes into it.
        </p>
        {/* The talk the paragraph above names, as it actually happens:
            evidence beside its claim. Clearance row in the ops ledger. */}
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={mmnTalk}
              alt="Alan giving the 'we're all middle management now' talk at a user group, the title slide up beside him"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">The talk, live: we're all middle management now</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="origin">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="origin" data-reveal>Where burningsuit comes from</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          burningsuit was my parents' Power BI training business. Stuart and Alison handed it over, and they're properly retired now, so it's me. Teaching is still at the heart of it: I run courses, and I work alongside teams on their own reports. The reason it exists hasn't changed.
        </p>
        <p data-reveal>
          As my mum put it, burningsuit was for "everyone else". Normal people, basically: not the nerds, not the people who are always online performing their work, just someone trying to get a job done. That's still who it's for.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={parents}
              alt="Stuart and Alison, who ran burningsuit before Alan, at a laptop together, Alison pointing at the screen"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">Stuart and Alison, the original Power BI people</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="dog">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="dog" data-reveal>And the dog</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p data-reveal>
          This is Zelda, who I'm told is Chief Morale Officer. She's no use at all with the DAX, but she's very good at making me get up and go outside now and then, which does more for the work than you'd think.
        </p>
        <figure class="figure-inset" data-reveal-fig>
          <div class="ph">
            <Picture
              src={zelda}
              alt="Zelda, a small black-and-tan dog, snuggled into a cream fleece blanket"
              formats={["avif"]}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              widths={[700, 1050, 1400]}
              sizes="(min-width: 56rem) 42rem, 100vw"
              quality={60}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="ph-note">Chief Morale Officer, on duty</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* The routes About ends with (website-page playbook): written out, not a
      chapter's reveal-gated prose, so the links never wait on an entrance. */}
  <section class="chapter" aria-labelledby="next">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="section-head" id="next" data-reveal>Where to go from here</h2>
      <ul class="routes routes-stack">
        <li><a class="route" href="/training/agentic-101/">The 101: half a day on handing Power BI work to an AI agent</a></li>
        <li><a class="route" href="/training/">The courses, for you or your team</a></li>
        <li><a class="route" href="/hour/">A free hour on a call, if it's your own reports</a></li>
      </ul>
      <Signoff reveal />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="thread thread-end" data-reveal-raw aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="talk-spacer"></div>
</BaseLayout>
